    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1153">

                <pb facs="1449" n="1439" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg1439"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3–247: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister-Counselor of Embassy in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Butterworth</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-03-02T19:00:00+08:00">March 2, 1947—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 2—3:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>418. Pending the receipt of a detailed report from Changchun on the capture by
                    the Communists of Assistant Military Attachés Rigg and Collins (ReEmbtel 408),
                    the Embassy decided not to make representations to Nanking Communist
                    representatives since it was hoped that there might be circumstances which would
                    facilitate their early release thru local action. It was considered undesirable,
                    on the basis of present scanty information to involve Yenan<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d1153-f1"/> in the case of which it was most probably completely
                    unaware. Any reference to Yenan would almost certainly have provoked
                    consequences likely to delay settlement.</p>
                <p>This morning the Embassy heard on the radio the War Department’s announcement,
                    the reasons for which are obscure. In these <pb facs="1450" n="1440"
                        xml:id="pg1440"/> circumstances, I took up the matter with Wang
                        Ping-nan,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d1153-f2"/> and asked him to request Yenan to
                    issue instructions for the immediate release of Rigg and Collins. He agreed to
                    communicate with Yenan forthwith.</p>
                <p>Colonel Dau, Ex. Officer MA,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d1153-f3"/> is flying to
                    Changchun March 3.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Butterworth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1153-f1" n="3" xml:id="d1153-f1-content">
